other people see our contributions, that they value those contributions, that they're glad to have us as a teammate. I've been impressed recently with how many professional football players talk about feeling loved by their teammates. And that's certainly a setting where you wouldn't expect to see people use that kind of terminology. You know, it's different. It doesn't have, well, obviously it doesn't have the romantic component. It may not even have the strong, emotional component, but it definitely has the part about being seen and understood and appreciated and the feeling that other people at work are really concerned about your well-being. You know, when people at work express condolences when someone in your family passes away,

